    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe behavioral redesign is wired up correctly and the comments/Javadoc were updated thoughtfully, but the change leaves two structural problems behind.\n\n**1. A third near-duplicate SHA-256\u2192hex helper (the main issue).**\nThe new `Owner.sha256Hex` (Owner.java:389-402) is essentially a third copy of a digest-and-hex-loop that already exists twice in the service layer:\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.sha256UpperHex(seed, length)` (ClinicServiceImpl.java:546-559) \u2014 live, called by `computeHouseholdId`.\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.hash8(owner)` (ClinicServiceImpl.java:322-338).\n\nAll three share the identical body: `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\")` \u2192 `digest(seed.getBytes(UTF_8))` \u2192 `StringBuilder` loop of `String.format(\"%02x\"/\"%02X\", b)` \u2192 `catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException) { throw new IllegalStateException(\"SHA-256 not available\", e) }`. The only axes of variation are hex case and truncation length. Adding a fourth-hand copy on a different class (the entity) rather than reusing one helper is exactly the kind of duplication a maintainer would flag. Extract a single shared hex-digest helper (parameterized by case and length, or produce full-length lower-hex and let callers slice/upper-case) and route all three call sites through it. That keeps the identity-key logic additive instead of scattering the same crypto boilerplate across `Owner` and `ClinicServiceImpl`.\n\n**2. Orphaned method + stale doc from the removed household-duplicate rule.**\nThis change removed the only caller of `existsOwnerInHousehold` (the deleted `householdExists` line in the controller). The method is now dead in both the interface (`ClinicService.java:68`) and the impl (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:461-470`), and its Javadoc still asserts it is \"the single basis for the household-duplicate rule\" \u2014 a rule the spec explicitly retires. Since duplicate detection is now the single identity key, remove `existsOwnerInHousehold` from the interface and impl (or, if it must stay, reconcile the Javadoc). Leaving a public service method whose documented purpose no longer exists is incoherent with the rest of the change.\n\nNot blocking: placing `soundex`/`soundexDigit` as static methods on `Owner` is consistent with the existing `fiscalYear`/`toBusinessDay` static utilities on that entity and used by both `getIdentityKey` and the service, so that location reads naturally for this codebase.",
